[Music]  the problem in society we have at the  moment  is everyone is afraid of making a  mistake what  may happen if we don't do anything  the pandemic has changed life as we know  it  but what comes next will our fast-paced  lives pick up again  will we continue to innovate regardless  of the cost  live limes profit driven  and globally connected  or has this pandemic sparked  irreplicable change  how will we live in the future after the  pandemic  hello and welcome to dw's coveted 19  special i'm stephen beardsley in berlin  well this week we're taking a look ahead  at life after the pandemic  from what it means for cities to  economies and even nutrition  a recent study has linked higher obesity  rates with higher covet death rates  the world obesity federation report says  covet death rates were 10 times higher  in countries where the population was on  average overweight  further almost 90 percent of cover  deaths occurred in countries with high  obesity rates  the report calls for people living with  obesity to have priority access to  vaccines  meanwhile the pandemic itself has  increased hunger and malnutrition around  parts of the world  take brazil for the first time a  whopping half of brazilians don't have  access to safe and nutritious food  that's according to ready pinson a local  food research group  lines are swelling by the day at soup  kitchens and food distribution points in  the country  experts say the sharp increase in food  insecurity  is due to job losses caused by the  pandemic  pauline shielbeck is assistant professor  at the london school of hygiene and  tropical medicine and she joins me from  london  paulina is good to have you on the show  what has the pandemic taught us about  past choices when it comes to nutrition  the pandemic has taught us that  that disruption or disruptive events in  in our lifestyle do lead to uh changes  in in  the foods that we eat and the choices  that we make uh so initial data shows  that  uh people certainly have changed their  diets in a pandemic  for the better and and worse as well for  some people that improved their diets  and for others it made their diets uh  worse  uh and what exactly that is so did it  was it that because they spent more time  at home because was it because  other things in the supermarket were on  offer  or they had more time to uh to cook  their meals for example  we don't exactly know yet but diets have  certainly changed  do you think that some of those obesity  rates we mentioned just previously that  that  may lead some people to rethink um  perhaps the way they're eating  does it give people time during the  pandemic what is it that makes people  have a rethink  um i think time is certainly uh  an issue uh there and what might be  really interesting is to see  uh to kind of isolate that uh group of  people that  uh started eating better started eating  uh more fruits and vegetables and  and and remember this comes in a um  in a food system where um or in a world  where  a very small proportion of people  actually adhere to  uh sufficient fruit and vegetables as uh  for the different guidelines in in  countries or by the who  um quite a small percentage adheres to  to that and you see that in a pandemic a  proportion of people have  very much increased their consumption of  healthy foods such as fruits and  vegetables and legumes  and reduced unhealthy foods whereas  others didn't so what would be really  important  and a kind of a lesson that we can take  out of the  the pandemic when going forwards in  terms of food systems and diets for the  future  is to see what were those barriers that  were  removed by the pandemic for these people  that led to these improvements of their  of their diets and can we use that to to  to learn a little bit  about what sort of leverage points we  have in the food system in supermarkets  in homes of people in  uh perhaps even further skills on how to  cook certain foods from  from scratch that would remove those  barriers for others as well so that they  could improve on their diets as well  it sounds like you believe that some of  these changed habits whether good or bad  could stay beyond the pandemic yes  absolutely i think people  uh certainly and then again we're  talking a little bit more about the  group that  uh experience an improvement of their  their diet  might also be the ones that kind of got  a little bit more in touch with  um uh what they're actually eating on a  on a  daily basis so there's less um running  out of the office going to the  supermarket and buy a ready-to-eat meal  and there's more uh cooking  from scratch so um you will be more  aware than  naturally how much salt goes in there uh  oils fats etc  and uh and a few more of the um  unhealthy  or the the foods that we eat too much of  we've also seen so yes  sorry i just want to say we've also seen  during this pandemic that  food supply itself has changed what is  that meant for nutrition in terms of  getting fresh food yeah so this is a  really  important lesson and and something that  perhaps some  uh some scientists have been uh warning  about for a while but our food system is  really quite  um uh fragile uh you see it in the uk  i'm most familiar with  the environment here but certainly also  in other countries in europe and in fact  actually everywhere uh globally and what  we see  is that um most countries also have um  uh they they have uh food stocks for  only a few days you very much dependent  on  other countries once the board is closed  that that really poses a problem to  um to stock supermarkets for for example  but we also see that disruptions and in  this case it was a pandemic but it could  also be natural disaster climate change  uh civil unrest in the that we're  sourcing from  uh that that would um uh could certainly  lead to  uh all the southern products not being  available in a supermarket or prices  going up  um and especially uh fruits and  vegetables  we do get them often from uh  countries that are vulnerable to to  these types of disruption  and that could ultimately lead for  example to increase prices of fruits and  vegetables in new supermarkets which  naturally then would uh  disproportionately  affect those that struggle a little bit  in buying their daily supermarket  i was gonna say that i'm sorry i was  just gonna say that the brexit also  apparently the uk has obviously been  changing some of those supply lines  as well i just want to ask how can we  better plan  food supply lines when we know what  we've known from the pandemic and from  brexit for example are there any lessons  there  yes absolutely i think mapping out uh  where everything is currently uh coming  from how much of that is for example  domestically  uh produced what sort of like um  storage and stocks do we uh do do we  have and are there certain pivotal  uh foods or food groups that are  particularly  um vulnerable or non-resilient in that  in that sense and and is there ways how  to inform  uh the consumers but also the producers  about these vulnerabilities and and see  how perhaps there are some substitutions  to make that to make food systems much  more  sustainable and make sure that the  supply is is resilient  really quick could it could it mean more  growing locally for example instead of  importing stuff yeah absolutely like  and especially because uh in a more  sustainable world we would be looking at  improving our uh plant-based food  consumption  um anyway so uh  so that would we would need to find ways  um to source uh these foods which could  be  um locally uh not to say that trade with  um lower income countries or so is a bad  thing per se of course their gdps depend  on on export with  countries in the global north as well  but certainly  um looking at ways how to minimize the  externalities for  for health but also for the environment  is certainly something that we could  um improve upon we'll have to leave it  there pauline shelby thank you very much  london school of hygiene and tropical  medicine  over to our science correspondent derek  williams now with a question that goes  straight to the gut  you hear a lot about the microbiome in  health what is it  and why is it so important  [Music]  the term microbiome describes the  entirety  of the microbial community that inhabits  an organism like a human it's made up  of a huge number of microorganisms  even conservative estimates say that  there are more  microbes living in or on you  then there are cells in your entire body  carrying your own  dna but because most microbes are much  smaller than body cells  if you put every one of them in your  microbiome on a scale  it'd only weigh a couple of kilos with  with most of the microbes  coming from the gut the average human  is home to over 10 000  microbial species many of which play for  example  vital roles in digestion and  over the last half a century scientists  have discovered that the makeup  and the balance of those species in an  individual's microbiome plays  a fundamental role in health and and can  be linked to to many medical conditions  just look for instance at a recent  large-scale study on  aging and the microbiome the authors  found  that in the elderly reduced populations  of common bacterial species in the gut  were associated with  overall better health aging people whose  bacterial balance remained more static  over time  didn't live as long on average although  it's  really tough to to clarify  cause and effect in a system as complex  as as the human microbiome links  to both health and illness have been  established in fields from  from allergy science to to  cardiovascular disease  to to neurological disorders like like  alzheimer's  it'll take a lot more research though to  pin down exactly in what ways  it's important to specific health issues  but  many common diseases might one day soon  be treated by tweaking a patient's  microbiome  [Music]  our science expert derek williams there  that's it for our show today thanks for  watching as always  stay safe and we'll see you again soon  [Music]  you

